															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/augmentedspartan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1775542); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1775542&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200996" alt="augmentedspartan" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1775542)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/augmentedspartan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1775542); return false;" >augmentedspartan</a></span> <span class="odate time_1576362626 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">14 Dec 2019 22:30</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4446553">
									

<p>This feels too close the what I dislike about AWCY? even if it's not the intended point. It seems overt anomalous art is always centered around &quot;modern art&quot; one way or another. I would like to see more SCP about anartists that use their abilities for something they enjoy. There's SCPs that have what would otherwise be anomalous art (movies, books, paintings, etc&#8230;) but that don't really explore the artistic part of it. The intentions of the artists, what it means to them, the process of creation, etc&#8230; would be interesting aspects of art to explore through SCPs that aren't depressing or dangerous. Maybe it's me forgetting articles or not reading the right ones, but a lot of the stuff that seems to get close to exploring artists tends to be kinda depressing or generally negative. A more positive outlook of art would be welcome.</p>
<p>SCP-4390 is about a guy making a real life version of a board game because he likes that stuff, and there's no dead participants. I find that type exploration of artists and art much more interesting than grabbing the cliched elitists &quot;modern artists&quot; and making them malicious.</p>
<p><a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/metacritique-two">Kalinin's Metacritique 2</a> does a way better job of explaining my point, and there's a quote that I really like.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Just imagine the things that artists could do if the strictures of what is possible are completely lifted. What if Michelangelo were not bound by the practical limitations of the dimensions and accessibility of the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel? What kind of journey could a Kiowa shaman provide if he had something more powerful than mere peyote to dispense? What wonders could the pharaohs have commanded if the laws of physics were optional? What kind of experience awaits the audience of a Noh school that has adapted its structure beyond the ordinary costuming and staging limitations of consensus reality?</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div>
</div>

